  "nameStory": "",
  "description": "Violet Rust (Puccinia violae) is a fungal pathogen belonging to the Pucciniaceae family that specifically infects violet plants. This rust fungus is commonly found throughout New England and can cause significant damage to wild and cultivated violet species.",
  "idTips": [
    {
      "label": "Pustule Color and Location",
      "description": "Orange to reddish-brown uredinia (spore-producing structures) appear on the undersides of violet leaves",
      "isPrimary": true
    },
    {
      "label": "Leaf Symptoms",
      "description": "Infected leaves develop yellow or chlorotic patches on the upper surface with corresponding rusty pustules underneath",
      "isPrimary": false
    },
    {
      "label": "Host Specificity",
      "description": "The fungus exclusively infects Viola species and related violets, making host identification helpful for confirmation",
      "isPrimary": false
    }
  ],
  "habitat": "Violet Rust thrives in moist environments with cool to moderate temperatures typical of New England forests, woodlands, and gardens where violets naturally grow.",
  "seasonality": {
    "spring": "",
    "summer": "",
    "fall": "",
    "winter": ""
  },
  "funFact": "Like many rust fungi, Puccinia violae has a complex life cycle involving multiple spore stages; however, it primarily spreads via uredospores throughout the growing season, making spring and early summer peak infection periods.",
  "confusionNotes": null,
